          %h2#lies_three_theorems Lie’s three theorems
          %p
            There is an obvious
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lie
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Lie
                  %mi Gp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Lie
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Lie \;\colon\; Lie Gp \to Lie Alg
          %p
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LieGroups"} LieGroups
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LieAlgebras"} LieAlgebras
            –
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+differentiation"} Lie differentiation
            – which sends any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            and every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+homomorphism"} group homomorphism
            of Lie groups to the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+homomorphism"} algebra homomorphism
            of Lie algebras.
          %p
            Lie’s three theorems can be understood as establishing salient properties of this functor. More exactly, Lie’s theorems provide a foundation establishing an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lie+groups"} local Lie groups
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89lie+Cartan"} Élie Cartan
            and others extended the theorems to give information on actual (“global”)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            via the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lie
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong Lie’s first theorem
                is purely local; see the
                %a{:href => "http://www.encyclopediaofmath.org/index.php/Lie_theorem"} Encyclopedia of Math
                for a statement. (Here one lacks a good notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
                for extending this to a global result.)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Lie II
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                be Lie groups with Lie algebras
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo =
                      %mi Lie
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = Lie(G)
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔥
                        %mo =
                        %mi Lie
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{h} = Lie(H)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+space"} simply connected
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝔤
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝔥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathfrak{g} \to \mathfrak{h}
                is a morphism of Lie algebras, then there is a unique morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : G \to H
                of Lie groups lifting
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo =
                        %mi Lie
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = Lie(F)
            %li
              %p
                %strong Lie III
                (Cartan-Lie theorem) The functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Lie
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong
                    is
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+surjective+functor"} essentially surjective on objects
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+space"} real
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
                there is a real Lie group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                such that
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi Lie
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} \cong Lie(G)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                which is simply connected.
          %p For a classical account see:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Duistermaat"}> Hans Duistermaat
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johan+A.+C.+Kolk"} Johan A. C. Kolk
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lie groups
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-56936-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-56936-4
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
            %p
              In his third theorem, Lie proved only the existence of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lie+group"} local Lie group
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%89lie+Cartan"} Élie Cartan
              = succeed "." do
                %em Cartan-Lie theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lie
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie
              from the category of
              %em local Lie groups
              to the category of finite dimensional real Lie algebras, and similarly the second Lie theorem establishes that this functor is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+faithful+functor"} fully faithful
              (so the two together establish that this functor is an
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Serre64"} Serre 1964
            %p
          %h2#restriction_to_simply_connected_lie_groups Restriction to simply connected Lie groups
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi LieGroups
                    %mi simpl
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LieGroups_{simpl}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LieGroups
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LieGroups
            consisting of simply connected Lie groups. Then the above implies that restricted to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi LieGroups
                      %mi simpl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LieGroups_{simpl}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lie
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lie
            becomes an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %h2#generalization_of_lies_theorems_to_lie_groupoids Generalization of Lie’s theorems to Lie groupoids
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+categorification"} horizontal categorification
            of Lie’s theorems for Lie groups leads to analogous statements for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoids
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi diff
                  %mo :
                  %mi LieGroupoids
                  %mo →
                  %mi LieAlgebroids
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} diff : LieGroupoids \to LieAlgebroids.
          %p
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoids
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroids
          %p
            In the case of Lie groupoids, the condition of a group being simply connected which plays an important role in the above statements is generalized to the condition that
            %em source fibers
            of the Lie groupoid (the preimages
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^{-1}(x)
            of the source map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s : C_1 \to C_0
            at every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in C_0
            of the Lie groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mi is
                  %mi source
                  %mo −
                  %mi simply
                  %mi connected
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mn 0
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (C is source-simply connected)
                  \\Leftrightarrow
                  (\forall x \in C_0 :
                  \\pi_1(s^{-1}(x)) = 0
                  ).
          %p
            %strong
              %em Lie II
              for Lie groupoids
            now reads exactly as
            %em Lie II
            for Lie groups, with “simply connected” replaced by “source simply connected”.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "Leibniz+algebra#LieThirdTheorem"} Lie’s third theorem for Leibniz algebras
          %h2#references References
          %h3#for_lie_groups_and_lie_algebras For Lie groups and Lie algebras
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Serre64
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"}> Jean-Pierre Serre
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lie Algebras and Lie Groups – 1964 Lectures given at Harvard University
                %strong 1500
                (1992) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-70634-2"}> doi:10.1007/978-3-540-70634-2
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sigurdur+Helgason"}> Sigurdur Helgason
                , Thm. 7.5 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential geometry, Lie groups and symmetric spaces
                %strong 34
                (2001) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/gsm-34"}> ams:gsm-34
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#for_lie_groupoids_and_lie_algebroids For Lie groupoids and Lie algebroids
          %p
            Lie II for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            was proven in
          %ul
            %li
              K. C. H. Mackenzie and P. Xu,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Integration of Lie bialgebroids
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              I. Moerdijk and J Mrčun,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On integrability of infinitesimal actions
          %p
            %strong
              %em Lie III
              for Lie groupoids
            does
            %em not
            hold in direct generalization:
          %p
            by the general mechanism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
            the space of morphisms of the source simply-connected
            %em topological
            groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            integrating a Lie algebroid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            is a
            %em quotient
            space. This quotient may fail to be a
            %em manifold
            due to singularities.
          %p
            On the precise conditions under which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroids"} Lie algebroids
            do have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            integrating them:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marius+Crainic"}> Marius Crainic
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rui+Loja+Fernandes"} Rui Loja Fernandes
              = succeed "," do
                %em Integrability of Lie brackets
              %strong 157
              2 (2003) 575-620 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.DG/0105033"}> arXiv:math.DG/0105033
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2003.157.575"} doi:10.4007/annals.2003.157.575
          %p
            Enlarging
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            to groupoids in the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+stacks"} etale stacks
            and smooth maps results in a Cartan–Lie theorem for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroids"} Lie algebroids
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hsian-Hua+Tseng"}> Hsian-Hua Tseng
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chenchang+Zhu"} Chenchang Zhu
              = succeed "," do
                %em Integrating Lie algebroids via stacks
              %strong 142
              (2006) 251–270 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/S0010437X05001752"}> doi:10.1112/S0010437X05001752
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            In particular, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebroid"} Lie algebroid
            can be integrated to an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoid"} Lie groupoid
            if and only if the integrating groupoid in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/etale+stacks"} etale stacks
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
          %p Comprehensive review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rui+Loja+Fernandes"}> Rui Loja Fernandes
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marius+Crainic"} Marius Crainic
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on Integrability of Lie Brackets
              %strong 17
              (2011) 1–107 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0611259"}> arxiv:math.DG/0611259
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2140/gtm.2011.17.1"} doi:10.2140/gtm.2011.17.1
          %p and in the introduction of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chenchang+Zhu"}> Chenchang Zhu
              ,
              %em Lie II theorem for Lie algebroids via stacky Lie groupoids
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0701024"}> arXiv:math/0701024
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#in_higher_lie_theory In higher Lie theory
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"} L-infinity algebras
            related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groups"} smooth infinity-groups
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Lie+theory"} higher Lie theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              (see also references at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integration
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christopher+L.+Rogers"}> Christopher L. Rogers
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jesse+Wolfson"} Jesse Wolfson
              %em
                Lie’s Third Theorem for Lie
                = succeed "-Algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
